  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Good match for the composition test: the envelope is transport-agnostic, keeps payload opaque, and explicitly assigns ordering, relay, and body-reference concerns to payload or wrapper protocols, so UDP-feed can remain a byte-delivery substrate. The main weakness is long-term evolution: unknown pCIDs are hard-rejected, making mixed-version carriage and archival interoperability brittle.",
    "strengths": [
      "Transport-agnostic envelope/payload split with explicit pCID dispatch and recursive wrapping.",
      "Deterministic CBOR and mandatory signature slots support strong local auditability.",
      "Avoids central binding or registry assumptions at the envelope layer."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "Unknown-pCID hard reject is brittle for forward compatibility and sparse-knowledge relays.",
      "Session semantics, replay handling, ordering policy, and group state are left to other protocols.",
      "The specimen is still draft and depends on external signature/proof protocols that are not frozen here."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Older peers may discard future session-layer traffic instead of preserving opaque evidence.",
      "Mandatory per-envelope signatures may over-constrain bootstrap or ultra-minimal traffic.",
      "Positional encoding plus external pCID knowledge can create sharp interoperability cliffs."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "How does this map to UDP-feed limits such as MTU, fragmentation, duplication, and reordering?",
      "May unknown-pCID envelopes be retained or relayed as opaque bytes without violating the hard-reject rule?",
      "What versioning path lets signature protocols and payload pCIDs evolve over decades?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
}
